    <div class="entry-content" set:html={`<p data-start="271" data-end="447">As a long-term <strong data-start="286" data-end="308">fava bean supplier</strong> working closely with food manufacturers, plant-based protein developers, and bulk ingredient buyers, we are often asked a simple question:</p><blockquote data-start="449" data-end="505"><p data-start="451" data-end="505"><em data-start="451" data-end="505">Are fava beans really as nutritious as people claim?</em></p></blockquote><p data-start="507" data-end="872">The short answer is <strong data-start="527" data-end="534">yes</strong>—but from a supplier’s perspective, nutrition facts are only meaningful when they are <strong data-start="620" data-end="694">consistent, measurable, and applicable in real production environments</strong>.<br data-start="695" data-end="698" />This article explains <strong data-start="720" data-end="750">fava beans nutrition facts</strong> based on scientific evidence and our hands-on experience in supplying <strong data-start="821" data-end="852">food-grade dried fava beans</strong> for commercial use.</p><hr data-start="874" data-end="877" /><div id="ez-toc-container" class="ez-toc-v2_0_85 counter-hierarchy ez-toc-counter ez-toc-grey ez-toc-container-direction"><div class="ez-toc-title-container"><p class="ez-toc-title" style="cursor:inherit">Table of Contents</p>
<span class="ez-toc-title-toggle"><a href="#" class="ez-toc-pull-right ez-toc-btn ez-toc-btn-xs ez-toc-btn-default ez-toc-toggle" aria-label="Toggle Table of Content"><span class="ez-toc-js-icon-con"><span class=""><span class="eztoc-hide" style="display:none;">Toggle</span><span class="ez-toc-icon-toggle-span"><svg style="fill: #999;color:#999" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="list-377408" width="20px" height="20px" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none"><path d="M6 6H4v2h2V6zm14 0H8v2h12V6zM4 11h2v2H4v-2zm16 0H8v2h12v-2zM4 16h2v2H4v-2zm16 0H8v2h12v-2z" fill="currentColor"></path></svg><svg style="fill: #999;color:#999" class="arrow-unsorted-368013" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="10px" height="10px" viewBox="0 0 24 24" version="1.2" baseProfile="tiny"><path d="M18.2 9.3l-6.2-6.3-6.2 6.3c-.2.2-.3.4-.3.7s.1.5.3.7c.2.2.4.3.7.3h11c.3 0 .5-.1.7-.3.2-.2.3-.5.3-.7s-.1-.5-.3-.7zM5.8 14.7l6.2 6.3 6.2-6.3c.2-.2.3-.5.3-.7s-.1-.5-.3-.7c-.2-.2-.4-.3-.7-.3h-11c-.3 0-.5.1-.7.3-.2.2-.3.5-.3.7s.1.5.3.7z"/></svg></span></span></span></a></span></div><nav><ul class='ez-toc-list ez-toc-list-level-1 ' ><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-2'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-1" href="#What_Are_Fava_Beans" >What Are Fava Beans?</a></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-2'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-2" href="#Fava_Beans_Nutrition_Facts_Per_100g" >Fava Beans Nutrition Facts (Per 100g)</a><ul class='ez-toc-list-level-3' ><li class='ez-toc-heading-level-3'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-3" href="#Protein_Content" >Protein Content</a></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-3'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-4" href="#Dietary_Fiber" >Dietary Fiber</a></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-3'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-5" href="#Vitamins_in_Fava_Beans" >Vitamins in Fava Beans</a></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-3'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-6" href="#Mineral_Profile" >Mineral Profile</a></li></ul></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-2'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-7" href="#Antioxidants_and_Bioactive_Compounds" >Antioxidants and Bioactive Compounds</a></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-2'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-8" href="#Other_Functional_Components_in_Fava_Beans" >Other Functional Components in Fava Beans</a></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-2'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-9" href="#Low_Fat_High_Nutritional_Density" >Low Fat, High Nutritional Density</a></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-2'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-10" href="#Why_Nutrition_Facts_Matter_to_Buyers" >Why Nutrition Facts Matter to Buyers</a></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-2'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-11" href="#Final_Thoughts_Nutrition_Is_Only_Valuable_When_Supply_Is_Reliable" >Final Thoughts: Nutrition Is Only Valuable When Supply Is Reliable</a></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-2'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-12" href="#FAQ" >FAQ</a><ul class='ez-toc-list-level-3' ><li class='ez-toc-heading-level-3'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-13" href="#1_Are_fava_beans_a_good_source_of_protein" >1. Are fava beans a good source of protein?</a></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-3'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-14" href="#2_How_much_protein_is_in_fresh_vs_dried_fava_beans" >2. How much protein is in fresh vs dried fava beans?</a></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-3'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-15" href="#3_Are_fava_beans_high_in_dietary_fiber" >3. Are fava beans high in dietary fiber?</a></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-3'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-16" href="#4_What_vitamins_are_found_in_fava_beans" >4. What vitamins are found in fava beans?</a></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-3'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-17" href="#5_What_minerals_do_fava_beans_contain" >5. What minerals do fava beans contain?</a></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-3'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-18" href="#6_Do_fava_beans_contain_antioxidants" >6. Do fava beans contain antioxidants?</a></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-3'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-19" href="#7_Are_fava_beans_low_in_fat" >7. Are fava beans low in fat?</a></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-3'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-20" href="#8_Are_fava_beans_suitable_for_food_manufacturing_and_industrial_use" >8. Are fava beans suitable for food manufacturing and industrial use?</a></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-3'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-21" href="#9_Do_fava_beans_contain_any_special_bioactive_compounds" >9. Do fava beans contain any special bioactive compounds?</a></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-3'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-22" href="#10_Why_is_supplier_quality_important_when_evaluating_fava_beans_nutrition_facts" >10. Why is supplier quality important when evaluating fava beans nutrition facts?</a></li></ul></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-2'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-23" href="#References" >References</a></li></ul></nav></div><h2 data-start="879" data-end="902"><span class="ez-toc-section" id="What_Are_Fava_Beans"></span>What Are Fava Beans?<span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h2><p data-start="904" data-end="1216"><a href="/dinweys-bulk-broad-bean-new-production/">Fava beans</a> (<em data-start="916" data-end="928">Vicia faba</em>), also known as broad beans, are one of the oldest cultivated legumes in the world. They are consumed both as <strong data-start="1039" data-end="1060">fresh green beans</strong> and <strong data-start="1065" data-end="1087">dried mature beans</strong>, with dried fava beans being the dominant form used in food manufacturing, plant protein processing, and bulk ingredient supply.</p><p data-start="1218" data-end="1304">From a supply chain standpoint, <strong data-start="1250" data-end="1270">dried fava beans</strong> are preferred because they offer:</p><ul data-start="1305" data-end="1404"><li data-start="1305" data-end="1339"><p data-start="1307" data-end="1339">Stable nutritional composition</p></li><li data-start="1340" data-end="1361"><p data-start="1342" data-end="1361">Longer shelf life</p></li><li data-start="1362" data-end="1404"><p data-start="1364" data-end="1404">Higher protein density per unit weight</p></li></ul><hr data-start="1406" data-end="1409" /><h2 data-start="1411" data-end="1451"><span class="ez-toc-section" id="Fava_Beans_Nutrition_Facts_Per_100g"></span>Fava Beans Nutrition Facts (Per 100g)<span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h2><p data-start="1453" data-end="1609">Nutritional values can vary slightly depending on variety and processing, but the ranges below are well supported by analytical data and published research.</p><h3 data-start="1611" data-end="1630"><span class="ez-toc-section" id="Protein_Content"></span>Protein Content<span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h3><p data-start="1632" data-end="1673">Fava beans are a <strong data-start="1649" data-end="1672">high-protein legume</strong>:</p><ul data-start="1675" data-end="1892"><li data-start="1675" data-end="1822"><p data-start="1677" data-end="1822"><strong data-start="1677" data-end="1697">Dried fava beans</strong>: approximately <strong data-start="1713" data-end="1745">20–25 g of protein per 100 g</strong>, with some varieties reaching close to <strong data-start="1785" data-end="1822">30% protein on a dry matter basis</strong></p></li><li data-start="1823" data-end="1892"><p data-start="1825" data-end="1892"><strong data-start="1825" data-end="1845">Fresh fava beans</strong>: approximately <strong data-start="1861" data-end="1892">8–10 g of protein per 100 g</strong></p></li></ul><p data-start="1894" data-end="2056">From our experience supplying fava beans to food processors, this protein level makes them an excellent <strong data-start="1998" data-end="2028">plant-based protein source</strong>, particularly suitable for:</p><ul data-start="2057" data-end="2172"><li data-start="2057" data-end="2087"><p data-start="2059" data-end="2087">Vegetarian and vegan diets</p></li><li data-start="2088" data-end="2135"><p data-start="2090" data-end="2135">Protein fortification in cereal-based foods</p></li><li data-start="2136" data-end="2172"><p data-start="2138" data-end="2172">Alternative protein formulations</p></li></ul><p data-start="2174" data-end="2339">Scientific studies confirm that fava bean protein has a <strong data-start="2230" data-end="2261">balanced amino acid profile</strong> and good digestibility compared to many other legumes (Multari et al., 2015).</p><hr data-start="2341" data-end="2344" /><h3 data-start="2346" data-end="2363"><span class="ez-toc-section" id="Dietary_Fiber"></span>Dietary Fiber<span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h3><p data-start="2365" data-end="2411">Fava beans are also rich in <strong data-start="2393" data-end="2410">dietary fiber</strong>:</p><ul data-start="2413" data-end="2452"><li data-start="2413" data-end="2452"><p data-start="2415" data-end="2452"><strong data-start="2415" data-end="2452">5–11 g of dietary fiber per 100 g</strong></p></li></ul><p data-start="2454" data-end="2488">This fiber content contributes to:</p><ul data-start="2489" data-end="2590"><li data-start="2489" data-end="2521"><p data-start="2491" data-end="2521">Improved intestinal motility</p></li><li data-start="2522" data-end="2547"><p data-start="2524" data-end="2547">Cholesterol reduction</p></li><li data-start="2548" data-end="2590"><p data-start="2550" data-end="2590">Prolonged satiety and glycemic control</p></li></ul><p data-start="2592" data-end="2752">In commercial food applications, fiber-rich legumes like fava beans are frequently used in <strong data-start="2683" data-end="2703">functional foods</strong> aimed at digestive health and weight management.</p><hr data-start="2754" data-end="2757" /><h3 data-start="2759" data-end="2785"><span class="ez-toc-section" id="Vitamins_in_Fava_Beans"></span>Vitamins in Fava Beans<span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h3><p data-start="2787" data-end="2880">Fava beans provide a broad range of vitamins, particularly <strong data-start="2846" data-end="2868">B-complex vitamins</strong>, including:</p><ul data-start="2882" data-end="3006"><li data-start="2882" data-end="2907"><p data-start="2884" data-end="2907">Vitamin B1 (Thiamine)</p></li><li data-start="2908" data-end="2935"><p data-start="2910" data-end="2935">Vitamin B2 (Riboflavin)</p></li><li data-start="2936" data-end="2950"><p data-start="2938" data-end="2950">Vitamin B6</p></li><li data-start="2951" data-end="2978"><p data-start="2953" data-end="2978"><strong data-start="2953" data-end="2976">Folate (Vitamin B9)</strong></p></li><li data-start="2979" data-end="2992"><p data-start="2981" data-end="2992">Vitamin C</p></li><li data-start="2993" data-end="3006"><p data-start="2995" data-end="3006">Vitamin K</p></li></ul><p data-start="3008" data-end="3263">Folate deserves special attention: it plays a critical role in <strong data-start="3071" data-end="3138">DNA synthesis, red blood cell formation, and neural development</strong>, making fava beans nutritionally valuable for a wide range of populations (USDA, supported by peer-reviewed nutrition data).</p><hr data-start="3265" data-end="3268" /><h3 data-start="3270" data-end="3289"><span class="ez-toc-section" id="Mineral_Profile"></span>Mineral Profile<span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h3><p data-start="3291" data-end="3347">Fava beans contain multiple essential minerals, such as:</p><ul data-start="3349" data-end="3680"><li data-start="3349" data-end="3387"><p data-start="3351" data-end="3387"><strong data-start="3351" data-end="3362">Calcium</strong> – supports bone health</p></li><li data-start="3388" data-end="3478"><p data-start="3390" data-end="3478"><strong data-start="3390" data-end="3398">Iron</strong> – essential for hemoglobin synthesis and prevention of iron-deficiency anemia</p></li><li data-start="3479" data-end="3559"><p data-start="3481" data-end="3559"><strong data-start="3481" data-end="3494">Potassium</strong> – helps maintain electrolyte balance and stable blood pressure</p></li><li data-start="3560" data-end="3629"><p data-start="3562" data-end="3629"><strong data-start="3562" data-end="3575">Magnesium</strong> – involved in energy metabolism and muscle function</p></li><li data-start="3630" data-end="3680"><p data-start="3632" data-end="3680"><strong data-start="3632" data-end="3640">Zinc</strong> – supports immune function and growth</p></li></ul><p data-start="3682" data-end="3878">From a supplier’s quality-control perspective, mineral content stability is influenced by <strong data-start="3772" data-end="3811">harvest maturity and drying methods</strong>, which is why standardized processing is critical for bulk supply.</p><hr data-start="3880" data-end="3883" /><h2 data-start="3885" data-end="3924"><span class="ez-toc-section" id="Antioxidants_and_Bioactive_Compounds"></span>Antioxidants and Bioactive Compounds<span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h2><p data-start="3926" data-end="4012">Beyond basic nutrition, fava beans contain several <strong data-start="3977" data-end="4000">bioactive compounds</strong>, including:</p><ul data-start="4014" data-end="4044"><li data-start="4014" data-end="4029"><p data-start="4016" data-end="4029">Polyphenols</p></li><li data-start="4030" data-end="4044"><p data-start="4032" data-end="4044">Flavonoids</p></li></ul><p data-start="4046" data-end="4262">These compounds exhibit <strong data-start="4070" data-end="4094">antioxidant activity</strong>, helping to neutralize free radicals and potentially reduce the risk of chronic diseases such as cardiovascular disorders and metabolic syndrome (Boukid et al., 2019).</p><hr data-start="4264" data-end="4267" /><h2 data-start="4269" data-end="4313"><span class="ez-toc-section" id="Other_Functional_Components_in_Fava_Beans"></span>Other Functional Components in Fava Beans<span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h2><p data-start="4315" data-end="4377">Fava beans also contain naturally occurring compounds such as:</p><ul data-start="4379" data-end="4442"><li data-start="4379" data-end="4400"><p data-start="4381" data-end="4400"><strong data-start="4381" data-end="4398">Phospholipids</strong></p></li><li data-start="4401" data-end="4416"><p data-start="4403" data-end="4416"><strong data-start="4403" data-end="4414">Choline</strong></p></li><li data-start="4417" data-end="4442"><p data-start="4419" data-end="4442"><strong data-start="4419" data-end="4440">L-DOPA (Levodopa)</strong></p></li></ul><p data-start="4444" data-end="4718">L-DOPA is of particular scientific interest due to its role in <strong data-start="4507" data-end="4529">dopamine synthesis</strong> and neurological function. While dietary intake alone is not therapeutic, studies confirm that fava beans are one of the richest natural food sources of this compound (Raina et al., 2014).</p><hr data-start="4720" data-end="4723" /><h2 data-start="4725" data-end="4761"><span class="ez-toc-section" id="Low_Fat_High_Nutritional_Density"></span>Low Fat, High Nutritional Density<span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h2><p data-start="4763" data-end="4850">Another advantage of fava beans is their <strong data-start="4804" data-end="4823">low fat content</strong>, making them suitable for:</p><ul data-start="4851" data-end="4908"><li data-start="4851" data-end="4868"><p data-start="4853" data-end="4868">Low-fat diets</p></li><li data-start="4869" data-end="4908"><p data-start="4871" data-end="4908">Heart-health-oriented food products</p></li></ul><p data-start="4910" data-end="5053">This combination—<strong data-start="4927" data-end="4964">high protein, high fiber, low fat</strong>—is why fava beans are increasingly used in <strong data-start="5008" data-end="5052">plant-based and clean-label formulations</strong>.</p><hr data-start="5055" data-end="5058" /><h2 data-start="5060" data-end="5099"><span class="ez-toc-section" id="Why_Nutrition_Facts_Matter_to_Buyers"></span>Why Nutrition Facts Matter to Buyers<span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h2><p data-start="5101" data-end="5219">From our role as a fava bean supplier, we see that buyers care less about theoretical nutrition values and more about:</p><ul data-start="5220" data-end="5347"><li data-start="5220" data-end="5250"><p data-start="5222" data-end="5250">Batch-to-batch consistency</p></li><li data-start="5251" data-end="5283"><p data-start="5253" data-end="5283">Dry matter protein stability</p></li><li data-start="5284" data-end="5347"><p data-start="5286" data-end="5347">Suitability for processing (milling, extrusion, fermentation)</p></li></ul><p data-start="5349" data-end="5496">Well-documented nutrition facts allow manufacturers to formulate products with <strong data-start="5428" data-end="5452">predictable outcomes</strong>, which is essential for scaling production.</p><hr data-start="5498" data-end="5501" /><h2 data-start="5503" data-end="5572"><span class="ez-toc-section" id="Final_Thoughts_Nutrition_Is_Only_Valuable_When_Supply_Is_Reliable"></span>Final Thoughts: Nutrition Is Only Valuable When Supply Is Reliable<span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h2><p data-start="5574" data-end="5772">Scientific research clearly shows that <strong data-start="5613" data-end="5655">fava beans are nutritionally excellent</strong>—rich in protein, dietary fiber, vitamins, minerals, and antioxidant compounds, while remaining naturally low in fat.</p><p data-start="5774" data-end="5887">However, from a supplier’s perspective, these nutrition facts only translate into real value when fava beans are:</p><ul data-start="5888" data-end="5965"><li data-start="5888" data-end="5910"><p data-start="5890" data-end="5910">Properly harvested</p></li><li data-start="5911" data-end="5930"><p data-start="5913" data-end="5930">Carefully dried</p></li><li data-start="5931" data-end="5965"><p data-start="5933" data-end="5965">Consistently graded and stored</p></li></ul><p data-start="5967" data-end="6042">That is why nutrition data and supply-chain expertise must go hand in hand.</p><hr data-start="6044" data-end="6047" /><h2 data-start="46" data-end="67"><span class="ez-toc-section" id="FAQ"></span>FAQ<span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h2><h3 data-start="69" data-end="120"><span class="ez-toc-section" id="1_Are_fava_beans_a_good_source_of_protein"></span><strong data-start="73" data-end="120">1. Are fava beans a good source of protein?</strong><span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h3><p data-start="122" data-end="430">Yes. Fava beans are considered a <strong data-start="155" data-end="178">high-protein legume</strong>. Dried fava beans typically contain <strong data-start="215" data-end="247">20–25 g of protein per 100 g</strong>, and in some varieties protein content can approach <strong data-start="300" data-end="329">30% on a dry matter basis</strong>. This makes them an excellent plant-based protein source, especially for vegetarian and vegan diets.</p><hr data-start="432" data-end="435" /><h3 data-start="437" data-end="497"><span class="ez-toc-section" id="2_How_much_protein_is_in_fresh_vs_dried_fava_beans"></span><strong data-start="441" data-end="497">2. How much protein is in fresh vs dried fava beans?</strong><span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h3><p data-start="499" data-end="760">Fresh fava beans usually contain <strong data-start="532" data-end="563">8–10 g of protein per 100 g</strong>, while dried fava beans are much more concentrated, providing <strong data-start="626" data-end="647">20–25 g per 100 g</strong> due to lower moisture content. This is why dried fava beans are preferred in food manufacturing and bulk supply.</p><hr data-start="762" data-end="765" /><h3 data-start="767" data-end="815"><span class="ez-toc-section" id="3_Are_fava_beans_high_in_dietary_fiber"></span><strong data-start="771" data-end="815">3. Are fava beans high in dietary fiber?</strong><span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h3><p data-start="817" data-end="1093">Yes. Fava beans contain approximately <strong data-start="855" data-end="892">5–11 g of dietary fiber per 100 g</strong>, which supports digestive health, helps regulate cholesterol levels, and contributes to satiety. Their fiber content is one reason they are widely used in functional and health-oriented food products.</p><hr data-start="1095" data-end="1098" /><h3 data-start="1100" data-end="1149"><span class="ez-toc-section" id="4_What_vitamins_are_found_in_fava_beans"></span><strong data-start="1104" data-end="1149">4. What vitamins are found in fava beans?</strong><span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h3><p data-start="1151" data-end="1405">Fava beans are rich in <strong data-start="1174" data-end="1196">B-complex vitamins</strong>, including <strong data-start="1208" data-end="1255">vitamin B1, B2, B6, and folate (vitamin B9)</strong>. They also provide <strong data-start="1275" data-end="1302">vitamin C and vitamin K</strong>, which play important roles in metabolism, red blood cell formation, immune function, and bone health.</p><hr data-start="1407" data-end="1410" /><h3 data-start="1412" data-end="1459"><span class="ez-toc-section" id="5_What_minerals_do_fava_beans_contain"></span><strong data-start="1416" data-end="1459">5. What minerals do fava beans contain?</strong><span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h3><p data-start="1461" data-end="1712">Fava beans contain several essential minerals such as <strong data-start="1515" data-end="1576">calcium, iron, potassium, magnesium, phosphorus, and zinc</strong>. These minerals support bone health, oxygen transport in the blood, electrolyte balance, immune function, and overall metabolic health.</p><hr data-start="1714" data-end="1717" /><h3 data-start="1719" data-end="1765"><span class="ez-toc-section" id="6_Do_fava_beans_contain_antioxidants"></span><strong data-start="1723" data-end="1765">6. Do fava beans contain antioxidants?</strong><span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h3><p data-start="1767" data-end="1984">Yes. Fava beans contain <strong data-start="1791" data-end="1821">polyphenols and flavonoids</strong>, which are antioxidant compounds that help neutralize free radicals and may reduce the risk of chronic diseases, including cardiovascular and metabolic disorders.</p><hr data-start="1986" data-end="1989" /><h3 data-start="1991" data-end="2028"><span class="ez-toc-section" id="7_Are_fava_beans_low_in_fat"></span><strong data-start="1995" data-end="2028">7. Are fava beans low in fat?</strong><span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h3><p data-start="2030" data-end="2297">Fava beans are naturally <strong data-start="2055" data-end="2069">low in fat</strong>, making them suitable for low-fat diets and heart-health-focused food formulations. Their nutritional profile combines <strong data-start="2189" data-end="2230">high protein, high fiber, and low fat</strong>, which is particularly attractive in plant-based food development.</p><hr data-start="2299" data-end="2302" /><h3 data-start="2304" data-end="2381"><span class="ez-toc-section" id="8_Are_fava_beans_suitable_for_food_manufacturing_and_industrial_use"></span><strong data-start="2308" data-end="2381">8. Are fava beans suitable for food manufacturing and industrial use?</strong><span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h3><p data-start="2383" data-end="2643">Yes. From a supplier’s perspective, dried fava beans are widely used in <strong data-start="2455" data-end="2543">plant protein production, functional foods, snacks, and bulk ingredient applications</strong> due to their stable nutritional composition, high protein density, and good processing performance.</p><hr data-start="2645" data-end="2648" /><h3 data-start="2650" data-end="2715"><span class="ez-toc-section" id="9_Do_fava_beans_contain_any_special_bioactive_compounds"></span><strong data-start="2654" data-end="2715">9. Do fava beans contain any special bioactive compounds?</strong><span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h3><p data-start="2717" data-end="2936">Fava beans naturally contain compounds such as <strong data-start="2764" data-end="2813">choline, phospholipids, and L-DOPA (levodopa)</strong>. L-DOPA is involved in dopamine synthesis and is one of the reasons fava beans are of scientific and nutritional interest.</p><hr data-start="2938" data-end="2941" /><h3 data-start="2943" data-end="3032"><span class="ez-toc-section" id="10_Why_is_supplier_quality_important_when_evaluating_fava_beans_nutrition_facts"></span><strong data-start="2947" data-end="3032">10. Why is supplier quality important when evaluating fava beans nutrition facts?</strong><span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h3><p data-start="3034" data-end="3316">Nutrition values are only reliable when the supply is consistent. Factors such as <strong data-start="3116" data-end="3193">harvest maturity, drying method, moisture control, and storage conditions</strong> directly affect protein, fiber, and mineral stability. A reliable supplier ensures batch-to-batch nutritional consistency.</p><hr /><h2 data-start="6049" data-end="6073"><span class="ez-toc-section" id="References"></span>References<span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h2><p data-start="6075" data-end="6336">Boukid, F., Zannini, E., Carini, E., &amp; Vittadini, E. (2019). Pulses as functional ingredients: A review of processing effects on nutritional and functional properties. <em data-start="6243" data-end="6284">Trends in Food Science &amp; Technology, 86</em>, 1–15.<br data-start="6291" data-end="6294" /><a class="decorated-link cursor-pointer" href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.tifs.2019.01.020" target="_new" rel="noopener nofollow" data-start="6294" data-end="6336">https://doi.org/10.1016/j.tifs.2019.01.020</a></p><p data-start="6338" data-end="6618">Multari, S., Stewart, D., &amp; Russell, W. R. (2015). Potential of fava bean (<em data-start="6413" data-end="6425">Vicia faba</em>) as future protein supply to partially replace meat intake in the human diet. <em data-start="6504" data-end="6563">Comprehensive Reviews in Food Science and Food Safety, 14</em>(5), 511–522.<br data-start="6576" data-end="6579" /><a class="decorated-link cursor-pointer" href="https://doi.org/10.1111/1541-4337.12146" target="_new" rel="noopener nofollow" data-start="6579" data-end="6618">https://doi.org/10.1111/1541-4337.12146</a></p><p data-start="6620" data-end="6840">Raina, A. P., Laskar, R. A., &amp; Khursheed, S. (2014). Quantification of L-DOPA in different cultivars of faba bean (<em data-start="6735" data-end="6747">Vicia faba</em> L.). <em data-start="6753" data-end="6795">Journal of Food Science and Nutrition, 2</em>(3), 89–95.<br data-start="6806" data-end="6809" /><a class="decorated-link cursor-pointer" href="https://doi.org/10.1002/fsn3.93" target="_new" rel="noopener nofollow" data-start="6809" data-end="6840">https://doi.org/10.1002/fsn3.93</a></p>`} />
